(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #90. A buffer grown through a `buffer` parameter used to
 stop at the buffer's first allocation: `_reallocate_buffer` freed the block
 it grew out of, the caller was left holding the freed pointer, and the
 caller's next read of its own buffer segfaulted. The parameter IS the
 caller's buffer, so every append through it is the caller's append, however
 far past the first allocation it grows.)

To 'pad out' with a buffer called sink.
    append "0123456789" to sink.

a buffer called journal is "start".
a number called appended is 0.
While appended is less than 600,
    'pad out' of journal,
    Set appended to appended add 1.

(Five seed bytes and six hundred appends of ten bytes each. The first
 allocation holds 4096 bytes, so the 410th append is the one that moves the
 block out from under the caller.)
Print "size: {journal's size}".
Print "first byte: {byte 1 of journal}".
Print "last byte: {byte 6005 of journal}".
